{
  "topic": "atari_buried",
  "ttsScript": "Atari was worth two billion dollars. The fastest-growing company in American history. One in three households owned one. Then they drove trucks into the New Mexico desert and buried millions of cartridges in a landfill. People blame E.T., the game made in five weeks. But E.T. wasn't the cause. It was the symptom. Atari had no quality control. Anyone could make a game. By nineteen eighty-two, there were more cartridges than customers. Revenue dropped from two billion to one hundred million in two years. The entire industry collapsed ninety-seven percent. In twenty fourteen, excavators dug up that landfill. The cartridges were real. A company worth billions, buried in a hole in the desert.",
